The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ret = dev_pm_opp_get_opp_count(cpu_dev);
if (ret < 0)
    return -EPROBE_DEFER;
```
Reason:
The early check for the CPU's OPP count occurs before confirming if the SoC is supported. If the SoC is unsupported, this check incorrectly leads to a deferred probe, causing an endless loop of retries without proper initialization of the driver.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
switch (product_id & EXYNOS_MASK) {
case 0xE5422000:
    probe_func = exynos5422_asv_init;
    break;
default:
    return -ENODEV;
}
```
Reason:
By checking the product ID of the SoC first and returning an error if it is unsupported, the code prevents the deferred probe from occurring on unsupported SoCs, ensuring that the driver only attempts to initialize on valid hardware.

